<section class="full" aria-label="The Core narrative">
  <h2 class="section-title">The Core</h2>

  <p class="muted">
    Most People Ops “AI” fails for the same reason dashboards fail:
    <b>everyone is arguing about what the numbers mean</b>.
    Different definitions. Different thresholds. Different owners. Different “truth.”
  </p>

  <p class="muted">
    <b>The Core fixes that.</b> It’s the shared contract that every tool, metric, and A[i]gent references —
    so decisions stay consistent as teams, systems, and workflows change.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <b>When The Core is missing:</b> teams ship isolated tools, debate definitions in meetings, and lose weeks to reconciliation.
    <br><b>When The Core is present:</b> signals route cleanly → actions trigger safely → humans stay accountable.
  </div>

  <h3>What lives here</h3>
  <p class="small">
    Not “documentation.” Operating rules. This is how a People Operating System stays auditable, repeatable, and trusted.
  </p>

  <div class="miniGrid miniGrid--4" aria-label="Core pillars">
    <div class="mini">
      <b>Definitions</b>
      <span>Shared language so “pipeline health” and “quality” mean the same thing everywhere.</span>
    </div>
    <div class="mini">
      <b>Logic</b>
      <span>How signals are calculated and how they should be interpreted in context.</span>
    </div>
    <div class="mini">
      <b>Thresholds</b>
      <span>Clear triggers for flags, recommendations, and escalation — no vibes-based decisions.</span>
    </div>
    <div class="mini">
      <b>Ownership</b>
      <span>Who can change what, why, and how changes roll out safely without breaking trust.</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="rule"></div>

  <p class="muted">
    <b>The point:</b> tools shouldn’t “create truth.” They should <b>execute against shared truth</b>.
  </p>

  <p class="small">
    If you want AI embedded in real workflows, The Core is how you avoid Franken-systems and keep decision logic stable.
  </p>

